### Floating-Point Precision & Zero-Quantity Position Risk

When calculating `residual`, floating-point subtraction (`position.position.quantity.as_f64() - filled_quantity.as_f64()`) can result in a tiny positive value (e.g., `1e-16`) due to precision limits, even when the position is logically fully filled.

Since `is_positive_finite(residual)` will evaluate to `true` for any value strictly greater than `0.0`, the code will proceed to construct a `Quantity` with this tiny value. However, when `Quantity::new` is called with the instrument's precision (e.g., `2` or `4` decimal places), the value will be rounded/truncated to `0.0`.

This results in returning `Some(residual_position)` where the position's quantity is `0.0`. A position with `0.0` quantity is logically flat, but wrapping it in `Some` transitions the strategy's exposure state to `ExposureState::Managed`, which can lead to invalid order submissions (e.g., trying to exit a zero-quantity position) and strategy lockups.

### Recommendation
Check if the constructed `Quantity` is strictly positive (i.e., its `as_f64()` value is greater than `0.0`) before returning the residual position.

```rust
        let residual = position.position.quantity.as_f64() - filled_quantity.as_f64();
        if !is_positive_finite(residual) {
            return None;
        }
        let residual_qty = Quantity::new(residual, position.position.quantity.precision);
        if residual_qty.as_f64() <= 0.0 {
            return None;
        }
        let mut residual_position = position.position.clone();
        residual_position.quantity = residual_qty;
        Some(residual_position)
